*Sample post. Replace it with your own writing in Keystatic.*

A month ago I decided to stop doing the thing I had been doing for a long time. Not dramatically, and not all at once, but on purpose. This is a short account of what the first few weeks have been like.

The first surprise was how much of my day had been arranged around a role rather than around me. Calendars, habits, even the way I introduced myself at dinner. Taking the role away left a lot of open space, and open space is less restful than it sounds.

The second surprise was how quickly small routines rushed in to fill it. A walk in the morning. Reading before checking anything. Writing a few lines every day, even when they were bad. None of these are new ideas, but they feel different when you choose them instead of fitting them in.

## Explaining myself less

When someone asks what I do, I've started saying "I'm figuring that out," and people are mostly kind about it. Some are curious. A few are a little envious, which I didn't expect.

What I miss is the feeling of being good at something in public. Starting over means being a beginner where people can see you, and that takes some getting used to. This website is partly a way of practising that.

## Next month

I want to finish one small project, read two books properly, and write here every week. I'll report back on how that goes, including the parts that don't.

> The open space is the point. Don't rush to fill it.

That's a note I wrote to myself in week two. I'm still working on it.
